diff --git a/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md b/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..30af217
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md
@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+# Inventory panel: baked images
+
+These are the visuals from the Figma inventory panel (`design/figma/inventory-panel.json`: screen
+`12:340`, Slot component set `20:1`) that get exported and uploaded as images. Everything else is
+built from UI objects (see the last section).
+
+None of them is uploaded yet. The code uses `rbxassetid://0` placeholders, kept in one place:
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UI/Assets.luau`. When an image is uploaded, replace its
+placeholder constant there. If you change a sheet's layout, update its rects in the same file.
+
+## Export rules for all three images
+
+- **Scale.** The UI is laid out for a 1080-pixel-tall screen and grows up to 2x on 4K screens
+  (`Screen.MaxScale`). Export everything at **2x** its size in the design.
+- **Size limit.** Keep every image at 1024 pixels or less per side. All three are.
+- **Edges.** Export PNG with alpha bleed ("dilate" or "solidify"): fill the transparent pixels
+  with the color of the nearest edge. Roblox turns transparent pixels black on upload, which leaves
+  dark fringes on scaled soft edges unless the edge color is carried into them.
+- **Owner.** Upload under the account or group that owns the experience so the game is allowed to
+  load the images.
+- **Source files.** Keep the exported PNGs next to the design, in `design/figma/exports/`, so a
+  later re-export starts from the same files.
+
+## 1. Hex pattern tile
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| What | The faint hexagon grid behind the panel's content: group `40:78` "Hex Pattern", made of hexagons 112 x 128 with a 2 px white center stroke, at 6% opacity. |
+| Why baked | It's a repeating pattern of about 35 vector outlines. One tiled image replaces all of them, and Roblox can't draw hexagon outlines from UI objects. |
+| Export | One seamless tile of **112 x 192** at 1x, exported at **224 x 384**. Draw a 112 x 192 frame with clipping on at the panel's top-left corner (x 600, y 350), place the hexagons in it so the pattern lines up with the panel, set them to **100% opacity and pure white**, and export the frame at 2x. The tile has to be exactly one repeat: hexagon columns repeat every 112 px, and rows repeat every 192 px (every other row is shifted 56 px). |
+| Asset | `Assets.HexPattern.Image` (placeholder `PLACEHOLDER_HEX_PATTERN`) |
+| Drawn as | `ImageLabel` "HexPattern" covering the panel, inset 2 px so it stays inside the panel's stroke, with its own `UICorner` (radius 14). `ScaleType = Tile`, `TileSize = 112 x 192` offsets, `ImageColor3` white, `ImageTransparency = 0.94` (the 6% opacity). Its color and strength stay adjustable without re-exporting. |
+
+## 2. Icon sheet: close and lock
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| What | The two glyphs: `Icon/Close` (`40:80`, 20 x 20, in the close button) and `Icon/Lock` (`40:30` and the other locked slots, 24 x 24). |
+| Why baked | They are vector glyphs, which UI objects can't draw. They always appear together (the close button is always visible, and the locks show whenever a block is locked), so they share one sheet and load together. |
+| Export | One **128 x 64** PNG made of two 64 x 64 cells, with **white glyphs** on transparent. Cell 1 (x 0 to 63): the close glyph at **40 x 40**, with its top-left at **(12, 12)**. Cell 2 (x 64 to 127): the lock glyph at **48 x 48**, with its top-left at **(72, 8)**. The empty margin in each cell keeps neighboring pixels from bleeding into a glyph when it scales. |
+| Asset | `Assets.Icons.Close` and `Assets.Icons.Lock` (placeholder `PLACEHOLDER_ICONS`), with rects Close offset (12, 12) size (40, 40), and Lock offset (72, 8) size (48, 48). |
+| Drawn as | `ImageLabel` with `ImageRectOffset` and `ImageRectSize` set to the rect, `ScaleType = Fit`, tinted with `ImageColor3 = Text/Primary` (245, 246, 250). Close is drawn at 20 x 20, centered in the 44 x 44 close button. Lock is drawn at 24 x 24, 10 px above the slot's center, only in the Locked state. |
+
+## 3. Block art sheet
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| What | The block picture in each slot ("Block Art" rectangles, 56 x 56), with the **drop shadow baked in** (0, 4 offset, 8 blur, black at 40%). Layers in screen `12:340`: Brick `40:0`, Dirt `40:4`, Grass `40:8`, Window `40:12`, Wood `40:16`, Stone `40:20`, Sand `40:24`, Ice `40:28`, Lamp `40:33`, Gold `40:38`. |
+| Why baked | It's raster art. A shadow that follows the shape of an image can't be drawn by `UIShadow`, which only shades rectangular boxes, so the shadow is part of the image. |
+| Export | Each block is one **80 x 80** piece at 1x (the 56 x 56 art centered, plus 12 px on each side for the shadow, which reaches 12 px below the art). Wrap each art layer in an 80 x 80 frame with no fill, no clipping, and the art centered, then export the frame at 2x (**160 x 160**). Export Ice, Lamp, and Gold at **full opacity**: the screen shows them at 40% because they're locked, and Roblox applies that fade itself. Put the ten pieces on one **1008 x 336** sheet as a grid of 6 columns by 2 rows with **168 x 168** cells. Each piece sits 4 px in from its cell's top-left. Order the cells left to right, then top to bottom: Brick, Dirt, Grass, Window, Wood, Stone / Sand, Ice, Lamp, Gold. |
+| Asset | `Assets.blockArt(name)` (placeholder `PLACEHOLDER_BLOCK_ART`). Rect for cell `i`: offset (`(i % 6) * 168 + 4`, `(i // 6) * 168 + 4`), size (160, 160). The cell for each block is set by name in `BLOCK_ART_CELLS`. |
+| Drawn as | `ImageLabel` "BlockArt", 80 x 80 at (8, 4) in the 96 x 96 slot, so the art itself lands at the design's (20, 16, 56 x 56). `ScaleType = Fit`, no tint. Locked slots set `ImageTransparency = 0.6`, which is the design's 40% content opacity. |
+
+Why one sheet instead of ten images: the panel always shows all ten blocks at once, so a single
+texture loads them together instead of one at a time. A new block type means adding a cell
+(there's room for two more in row 2) and re-uploading the sheet under a new id. A block with no
+cell yet shows an empty slot instead of an error. If the block list grows well past twelve, or the
+art changes often, switch to one image per block.
+
+## Built from UI objects, not baked
+
+So you don't spend time exporting these:
+
+| Visual | Built with |
+|---|---|
+| Panel fill, 16 px corners, 2 px inside stroke | `Frame` + `UICorner` + `UIStroke` (`Inner`) |
+| Panel drop shadow (0, 12, blur 32, 45%) | `UIShadow` |
+| Panel background blur (24) | Approximated: fill made more opaque (92% to 96%). Roblox can't blur only what's behind one frame. |
+| Slot gradient, 12 px corners, 1 px stroke | `UIGradient` + `UICorner` + `UIStroke` |
+| Selected slot: 3 px gold outside stroke and glow | `UIStroke` (`Outer`) + `UIShadow`, switched on by state |
+| Close button's circle and 8% white fill | `UICorner` + background transparency |
+| Equip button gradient and inner shadow | `UIGradient` + `UIShadow` (`Inset`) |
+| All text and the text shadows | `TextLabel` with Builder Sans + `UIShadow` in text mode |
